# The drake parser reads infix operators by fixity, and a lambda — Dev, 2026-09-21 ("I know that I want
# the generator to see an expression tree where ever one exists in drake, rather than require the
# generator parse arbitrarily complex expressions"; "None of the arrows that can be used in drake seem to
# be given operator precedence in your table. For me that would solve the tree-form error example you
# gave"; and, on `=>` staying out of the table, "Yes, if by 'stays out' you mean it is generated for Scala
# based on what is in the expression tree in json"). On 912e4e8 (624 / 44 green). GitHub #61, point one.
#
# ONE CONCEPT: the operator layer. A value's token run is split FLAT at every declared infix operator and
# reassociated by fixity — Haskell's Prelude table, since drake takes Haskell's semantics: `* / %` 7 left,
# `+ -` 6 left, `++` 5 right, the six comparisons 4 non-associative, `&&` 3 right, `||` 2 right, and `->`
# at 0, BELOW every other row. The arrow's place is Dev's correction and what made the lambda part of the
# same step: a lambda `\p1 p2 -> body` is a token opening with `\` in operand position, its parameters up
# to the arrow, its body everything after, and because the arrow is the loosest operator the body takes
# the whole conjunction to its right — the wrong-scope tree that the whole-run rule had been guarding
# against cannot arise. `++`, `&&` and `||` are flat variadic nodes; a chained non-associative operator
# (`a == b == c`) is an error at parse, as in Haskell. `++` leaves the reserved set: a span runs to the
# next KEYWORD and the operators inside it are read by fixity, so `concatenation` and `operand` at the
# cursor are gone and `a == b ++ c` associates by the table rather than by where the cursor stopped.
# THE WHOLE-RUN RULE, kept for its honest remainder: a run trees only when every operand is ONE token or
# the lambda; a run with a several-token operand — a host `if`, a `new`, a block, a typed binder outside
# a lambda — stays the raw leaf it was, so the generator never receives a tree with the wrong scope and
# the loss report keeps measuring it. A parenthesized group is still a leaf (`(x)` is not a
# sub-expression; that, with minimal parenthesization in the renderers, is the next step). `=>` is never
# a drake token: it is the Scala target's spelling of the `\` node.
#
# MOVED: Drake.scala — `fixity`, `flattened`, `expression` (the flat split and the lambda), `lambdaOf`,
# `resolve` (precedence climbing), `infixNode`; `parseValue` is `value(span(c))`; the emitter spells every
# table operator. DracoGenerator.expression spells the same set. Expression.rootNames: a lambda BINDS its
# parameters (the body's roots less them). DrakeParseTest: one structural test — the Primes guard, the
# SelfDeclaration disjunction, a lambda over a conjunction, the engine rendering, the bound parameter,
# and the refused chain. CORPUS: every lambda written in the host's form converts to `\p -> body` —
# RuleAspect, DracoAspect (three), DomainAspect, CodecAspect, DomainDictionary, TypeDictionary (two),
# DefinitionPath (four), CLI's `main` (a typed binder as a leaf), aerial Consumer — and CLI's command map
